+A non-secure multiline input can enter the owned document backend only when
+Core supplies its internal fixed-viewport proof and a genuinely accepted
+controlled baseline contains at least 65,536 UTF-8 bytes. Positive measured
+dimensions alone are not that proof: auto height, unresolved width, unsupported
+row ancestry and content-size measurement retain the system path. Native edits
+or paste crossing the threshold, equal controlled echoes and metadata/frame
+updates do not migrate an active system editor. Once admitted, shorter content
+keeps the document owner. Withdrawing the fixed-viewport hint transfers complete
+text, edit count, selection and focus back to the system editor; this explicit
+backend change resets native undo history.
+
+The document owner keeps the complete editable source and document-wide UTF-16
+coordinates. A dedicated worker lays out natural paragraphs, retains a bounded
+paragraph LRU and returns viewport pixels and local geometry. It does not feed
+a prefix to TextArea or allocate a full-document-height bitmap. A long single
+paragraph still requires that paragraph's native layout. The API-20 path wires
+IME, focus, selection, clipboard menus and value/placeholder accessibility
+metadata; it does not provide system TextArea autofill or auto-capitalization.
+The optional API-23 editable accessibility provider adds full text/selection
+operations. These paths require device acceptance; host model tests do not prove
+IME, clipboard, accessibility or large-document performance parity.
+
+The document caret has a private native Custom child inside the input's native
+owner, outside the logical/Core tree. It measures the owner's complete frame,
+lays out at its origin, inherits transforms and clipping, and has no hit testing,
+focus or accessibility semantics. The parent draws viewport pixels, selection
+and composition geometry; caret draw records only the clipped caret. A blink
+dirties that child alone without scheduling layout, worker work, wrapper metadata
+or a parent bitmap redraw. Content, selection and caret-style changes invalidate
+the appropriate layers. Context suspension hides the caret while retaining its
+layer for resumption; retirement removes and disposes only that child, retaining
+failed cleanup for retry. Native display-list behavior and frame cost still
+require device profiling.
+
+Window keyboard routing inside an overlay is restricted to the top Modal's
+actually focused owned document or secure editor. Its logical Modal identity
+must match the focused editor's recorded scope. Application keymaps and generic
+text-selection shortcuts do not pass through a Modal to background content;
+ordinary system editors leave their keys to ArkUI. A generic alert overlay,
+a nested Modal without the matching focused editor, or a stale/cleared focus
+blocks this owned-editor route. Closing or deactivating an input clears its
+scope. This does not change the Modal transition or restore background shortcuts.
+
+`secure && multiline` separately uses NSH's Rust password model, password IME
+and masked raster. Secure focus requires the actual window identity; key-filter
+readiness is independent. Native editor, WebView, Video and Scroll accessibility
+trees remain with their native owners. Optional providers represent eligible
+semantic leaves and restore suppressed metadata on retirement.
 
